    "title": "pandas.Index.identical — pandas 3.0.5 documentation",
    "status": 200,
    "markdown": "# pandas.Index.identical#\n\n- \nfinal Index.identical(*other* )[source]#\n- Similar to equals, but checks that object attributes and types are also equal. \n  - Parameters:\n    - **other** Index\n    - The Index object you want to compare with the current Index object.\n  - Returns:\n    - bool\n    - If two Index objects have equal elements and same type True, otherwise False.\n See also \n  - `Index.equals`\n  - Determine if two Index object are equal.\n  - `Index.has_duplicates`\n  - Check if the Index has duplicate values.\n  - `Index.is_unique`\n  - Return if the index has unique values.\n Examples >>> idx1 = pd.Index([\"1\", \"2\", \"3\"]) >>> idx2 = pd.Index([\"1\", \"2\", \"3\"]) >>> idx2.identical(idx1) True >>> idx1 = pd.Index([\"1\", \"2\", \"3\"], name=\"A\") >>> idx2 = pd.Index([\"1\", \"2\", \"3\"], name=\"B\") >>> idx2.identical(idx1) False",
    "markdown_length": 823,
